  "worked_solution": "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the mean can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot {m}_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class, and\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each class and the products of the midpoints and frequencies.\n\n| Waiting time (minutes) | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${f}_{i}\\cdot {m}_{i}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $4$ | $5$ | $20$ |\n| $10\\le x<15$ | $6$ | $12.5$ | $75$ |\n| $15\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $17.5$ | $122.5$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $3$ | $25$ | $75$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFinally, we sum the values in the last column and divide by $20$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & ≈\\frac{20+75+122.5+75}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{292.5}{20} \\\\  & ≈14.6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
